{
 "answer": "the drift split was already exposure-matched. On both panels a flat arm held at each gated arm's own average weight reproduces E226's reading to within 0.000000 bp/yr (primary) and 0.000000 bp/yr (basket), because the always-on null pays no out-of-sample turnover (0.000 / 0.000 per year) and its excess is therefore exactly 1.0 x (mean OOS return - IS mean) -- so no arm changes sign out of 4 comparable arms on the primary panel and 4 on the basket. That is the finding, and it cuts against the E226 caveat rather than confirming it: a large gate-specific residual on the basket is not an attribution artifact, it is the arithmetic of charging an arm a share of a benchmark that moves a lot. On the primary panel binary_low leaves -1082.9 bp/yr of own excess against a null that lost 1645 bp/yr, so the residual is what is left of the rule after that charge -- the benchmark named the movement, it did not manufacture the gain.",
